---
/** Method: every rule the calculators apply, with its source and the checks run on it. Figures from params-2026.json and data.gov.sg. */
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { srcLink, table, sourceList } from '../../lib/helpers';
import { P, roadTaxAnnual } from '../../lib/engine/car-cost';
import { FETCHED, LATEST, FIRST, EXERCISES, exName, day, money, pct, PQP_NEXT, PQP_OFFICIAL_LAST, monthYear, PARAMS_READ } from '../../lib/data';
import { breadcrumbSchema, webPageSchema } from '../../lib/seo';
const lang = 'en';
const path = route('method', lang);
const title = 'COE Cost Method 2026: ARF, VES, PARF, Road Tax Rules';
const description = 'How COE Cost works in 2026: the LTA rules each calculator applies, the data.gov.sg COE results it reads, the PQP rule, checks against LTA examples, limits.';
const s = (k: string, t: string) => srcLink(k, lang, t);
const m = (x: number) => money(x, lang);
const p = (x: number) => pct(x, lang, 0);
const arfRows = (rg: 'from2023' | 'pre2023') => { let prev = 0; return P.arf[rg].map(([up, r]) => { const row = [up === null ? `Above ${m(prev)}` : `${m(prev)} to ${m(up)}`, p(r)]; prev = up ?? prev; return row; }); };
const parfRows = [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5].map((k) => [k === 0 ? 'Up to 5 years' : `Over ${4 + k} to ${5 + k} years`, p(P.parf.pre2023.rates[k]), `${p(P.parf.y2023to2026.rates[k])} (cap ${m(P.parf.y2023to2026.cap)})`, `${p(P.parf.from2026.rates[k])} (cap ${m(P.parf.from2026.cap)})`]);
const ves = (['A', 'B', 'C1', 'C2', 'C3'] as const).map((b) => [b, m(P.ves[2026][b]), m(P.ves[2027][b])]);
---
<PageLayout sources={sourceList(['lta-tax', 'lta-arf', 'lta-ves', 'lta-coe', 'lta-renewal', 'lta-roadtax', 'lta-rebate', 'lta-parf-2026', 'mas-loans', 'iras-gst', 'dgs-coe', 'dgs-pqp', 'sodl'], lang)} title={title} description={description} lang={lang} jsonLd={[webPageSchema({ name: 'Method', description, path, lang }), breadcrumbSchema([{ name: 'Home', path: route('home', lang) }, { name: 'Method', path }])]} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Method' }]}>
  <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <h1>How the figures are built</h1>
    <p>Two kinds of figures appear on this site. COE results and the prevailing quota premium are data: a script reads, on data.gov.sg, the Land Transport Authority’s COE results and the Singapore Department of Statistics PQP series (source: LTA) after each bidding exercise ({EXERCISES.length} exercises, from {exName(FIRST, lang)} to {exName(LATEST, lang)}, read on {day(FETCHED, lang)}). Taxes and rebates are rules: they were read on LTA’s pages on {day(PARAMS_READ, lang)}, written into one parameter file and applied by a calculation engine covered by automated tests. Nothing you type leaves your browser.</p>
    <h2>Upfront cost of a new car</h2>
    <p>The <Fragment set:html={s('lta-tax', 'vehicle tax structure')} /> adds, on top of the open market value (OMV) assessed by Singapore Customs: an excise duty of {p(P.exciseDutyRate)} of the OMV; GST of {p(P.gstRate)} on the OMV plus excise (<Fragment set:html={s('iras-gst', 'IRAS')} />); the registration fee of {m(P.registrationFee)}; the additional registration fee (ARF) in tiers; the VES rebate or surcharge and, for an electric car registered in 2026, the EEAI; and the COE premium. An imported used car pays a surcharge of {m(P.usedImportSurcharge)}. The dealer’s margin, insurance and accessories come on top and are not published by anyone.</p>
    <Fragment set:html={table(['OMV slice', 'ARF rate'], arfRows('from2023'), 'ARF tiers for COEs obtained from the 2nd exercise of February 2023.', ['l', 'r'], lang)} />
    <Fragment set:html={table(['OMV slice', 'ARF rate'], arfRows('pre2023'), 'ARF tiers for COEs obtained before that exercise.', ['l', 'r'], lang)} />
    <p>The ARF is rounded up to the dollar; on four lines of LTA’s own car cost list the engine lands on the same dollar. VES bands depend on the worst of five pollutants; the calculators ask for the band, and can only suggest one from CO2 alone. After rebates the ARF cannot fall below {m(P.minArf)}, except for fully electric cars registered up to {P.evNoMinArfTo}, which have no minimum (<Fragment set:html={s('lta-ves', 'emission schemes')} />).</p>
    <Fragment set:html={table(['VES band', 'Registered in 2026', 'Registered in 2027'], ves, 'Negative = rebate off the ARF, positive = surcharge.', ['l', 'r', 'r'], lang)} />
    <h2>COE results and the PQP</h2>
    <p>For each exercise and category the open data gives the quota, the bids received, the successful bids and the quota premium, which every successful bidder pays (<Fragment set:html={s('lta-coe', 'how bidding works')} />). Category A covers non-electric cars up to {P.catA.maxCc} cc and {P.catA.maxKw} kW and electric cars up to {P.catA.evMaxKw} kW (COEs from May 2022); category B the rest; C goods vehicles and buses; D motorcycles; E is open to all but motorcycles.</p>
    <p>The prevailing quota premium is the moving average of the premiums of the last three months in which bidding took place, rounded up to the dollar (<Fragment set:html={s('lta-renewal', 'COE renewal')} />). Recalculating it from the open data gives the published series for every month since 2002 without a single gap. The PQP for {monthYear(PQP_NEXT.appliesTo, lang)}, built on the premiums of {PQP_NEXT.basedOn.map((x) => monthYear(x, lang)).join(', ')}, is shown as our calculation until the official series, which currently ends with {monthYear(PQP_OFFICIAL_LAST, lang)}, catches up. Renewing for 10 years costs the PQP; for 5 years, half of it rounded up.</p>
    <h2>Road tax</h2>
    <p>Six-monthly road tax follows the formulas on LTA’s <Fragment set:html={s('lta-roadtax', 'road tax page')} />, multiplied by {P.roadTax.factor}: by engine capacity for petrol cars, by maximum power for electric cars plus an additional flat component of {m(P.roadTax.evAfcSixMonthly)} every six months, and the higher of the two for hybrids. Cars over 10 years old pay {p(0.1)} more, rising by ten points a year to {p(0.5)} beyond 14 years. A 1,598 cc car pays {money(roadTaxAnnual({ fuel: 'petrol', cc: 1598 }).total, lang, 2)} a year.</p>
    <h2>PARF and COE rebates</h2>
    <Fragment set:html={table(['Age at deregistration', 'COE before Feb 2023', 'Feb 2023 to Feb 2026', 'From Feb 2026'], parfRows, 'PARF rebate as a share of the ARF actually paid, by the exercise in which the COE was obtained.', ['l', 'r', 'r', 'r'], lang)} />
    <p>The PARF rebate is computed on the ARF paid after VES and EEAI (<Fragment set:html={s('lta-rebate', 'PARF and COE rebates')} />; new schedule in the <Fragment set:html={s('lta-parf-2026', 'news release of 12 February 2026')} />). The COE rebate is the premium paid times the unused months over 120; unused months are counted in whole calendar months from the day after deregistration, then the remaining days as thirtieths of a month. LTA’s worked example ($16,897, 29.1 months, $4,097) is one of the engine’s tests, as are the two examples of the February 2026 release.</p>
    <h2>Cost per year</h2>
    <p>Cost per year = (price paid − PARF rebate − COE rebate at the end + road tax and the running costs you enter × years) ÷ years. It assumes the car is deregistered at the end of the period; a car sold on the used market fetches more than its rebates, by an amount nobody publishes.</p>
    <h2>Car loans</h2>
    <p>The <Fragment set:html={s('mas-loans', 'Monetary Authority of Singapore')} /> caps a car loan at {p(P.loans.ltvLow)} of the purchase price (taxes and COE included) when the OMV is up to {m(P.loans.lowOmv)}, and {p(P.loans.ltvHigh)} above it, over {P.loans.maxYears} years at most. Banks quote flat rates; the calculator converts them into an effective yearly rate.</p>
    <h2>Licence and attribution</h2>
    <p>Contains information from COE Bidding Results / Prices (Land Transport Authority) and Motor Vehicle Quota, Quota Premium And Prevailing Quota Premium, Monthly (Singapore Department of Statistics, source: Land Transport Authority), accessed on 24 October 2026 from data.gov.sg, made available under the terms of the <Fragment set:html={s('sodl', 'Singapore Open Data Licence version 1.0')} />. This site is not affiliated with or endorsed by LTA. LTA’s, MAS’s and IRAS’s web pages are cited by organisation, title, address and date of reading, without a link; their text, tables and model-by-model car cost list are not reproduced.</p>
    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <p>No forecast of the next premium. No OMV per model. No insurance or petrol price. Off-peak cars, taxis, company cars and goods vehicles have rules of their own that the calculators do not cover. A change announced in Budget 2027 or after LTA’s consultation on COE categories will be applied the day it is published.</p>
  </article>
</PageLayout>
